---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Diagram from '../components/Diagram.astro';

const faq = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What happens if my domain does not have a CAA record?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "A domain with zero CAA records permits any publicly trusted Certificate Authority to issue an SSL/TLS certificate provided the requester passes standard domain validation (HTTP-01 or DNS-01). Publishing a CAA record is optional, but strongly recommended to lock issuance down to your chosen CA."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How do I allow multiple Certificate Authorities in a CAA record?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "To authorize more than one Certificate Authority (for example, both Let's Encrypt and DigiCert), create separate CAA record rows on the same hostname: one row with 0 issue \"letsencrypt.org\" and a second row with 0 issue \"digicert.com\". Never put multiple CA names inside a single CAA record."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Does a CAA record on my root domain (@) automatically protect all of my subdomains?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes, in practice CAA records automatically inherit downward to all subdomains via tree climbing (sub.example.com inherits example.com's CAA rules). Adding a single CAA record directly onto a subdomain completely replaces the root domain's CAA list for that subdomain rather than merging with it."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Why did Let's Encrypt fail with \"SERVFAIL looking up CAA\" when I don't even have a CAA record?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "In real-world troubleshooting, this error means Let's Encrypt queried your domain for a CAA record and received a DNS error (SERVFAIL or timeout). Broken DNSSEC DS records at your registrar or unresponsive nameservers trigger this error. Because CAs verify whether a CAA record exists, a DNS SERVFAIL blocks issuance until administrators repair the DNS or DNSSEC chain."
      }
    }
  ]
};

const breadcrumb = [
  ["Home", "/"],
  ["DNS Guide", "/dns/"],
  ["Record Types", "/dns-records/"],
  ["CAA Record"]
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="CAA Record: What Certificate Authority Authorization Is, Syntax, and Examples"
  description="Learn what a DNS CAA record is, how issue, issuewild, and iodef tags restrict SSL certificate issuance, and how to configure CAA records for your domain."
  schema={[faq]}
  breadcrumb={breadcrumb}
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/dns/" class="hover:text-accent">DNS Guide</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/dns-records/" class="hover:text-accent">Record Types</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">CAA Record</span>
    </nav>

    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">
      CAA Record: What Certificate Authority Authorization Is, Syntax, and Examples
    </h1>

    <div class="pd-answer mt-6" data-vs="answer">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
        A <abbr class="pd-term" data-tip="CAA record: Certificate Authority Authorization resource record (IANA Type 257) specifying which CAs may issue certificates">CAA record</abbr> is <strong class="text-ink">a DNS security record that lets domain owners specify which Certificate Authorities (CAs) are allowed to issue SSL/TLS certificates for their domain</strong>. Every public CA checks CAA records before issuing a certificate. The 3 fields control single-domain, wildcard, and violation reporting.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Diagram name="resolution" caption="Certificate Authority Authorization flow: Certificate Authorities query CAA records from authoritative DNS servers to validate certificate issuance permissions before signing" />

    <nav class="pd-toc my-8" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-accent">On this page</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#what-is-a-caa-record-and-what-do-its-3-fields-mean-flags-tag-and-value">What is a CAA record and 3 fields</a></li>
        <li><a href="#official-caa-domain-identifiers-for-8-major-certificate-authorities-and-cdns">Official CAA strings for major CAs and CDNs</a></li>
        <li><a href="#how-do-certificate-authorities-evaluate-caa-records-tree-climbing-inheritance-and-overrides">How CAs evaluate CAA records (tree climbing)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#5-copy-paste-caa-record-examples-for-every-security-scenario">5 copy-paste CAA record examples</a></li>
        <li><a href="#why-do-caa-lookups-cause-ssl-certificate-renewal-failures-3-common-pitfalls">Why CAA lookups cause certificate renewal failures</a></li>
        <li><a href="#how-to-add-and-check-your-domains-caa-records-dig-nslookup-and-browser-doh">How to add and check CAA records</a></li>
        <li><a href="#frequently-asked-questions">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2 id="what-is-a-caa-record-and-what-do-its-3-fields-mean-flags-tag-and-value">What Is a CAA Record and What Do Its 3 Fields Mean? (Flags, Tag, and Value)</h2>
      <p>
        A Certificate Authority Authorization (CAA) resource record (IANA Type 257, standardized in RFC 8659 to replace RFC 6844) <strong class="text-ink">acts as a public DNS allowlist that prevents unauthorized or mis-issued SSL/TLS certificates</strong>. Under mandatory CA/Browser Forum rules enforced since September 8, 2017, every publicly trusted <abbr class="pd-term" data-tip="Certificate Authority: trusted cryptographic organization that verifies domain ownership and signs public SSL/TLS certificates">Certificate Authority</abbr> queries a domain's CAA records before issuing certificates, aborting issuance if the CA's identifier is unauthorized across 3 fields: Flags (<code>0</code> or <code>128</code>), Tag (<code>issue</code>, <code>issuewild</code>, or <code>iodef</code>), and Value. The table below defines these fields and standardized property tags.
      </p>

      <p class="text-sm font-medium text-ink-dim">
        Standard zone syntax illustrates single-domain, wildcard, and reporting records with standard <abbr class="pd-term" data-tip="TTL: Time to Live in seconds specifying resolver caching duration">TTL</abbr> intervals:
      </p>

      <pre class="overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-line bg-panel/60 p-4 font-mono text-xs text-ink leading-relaxed"><code>; Host / Domain   TTL    Class  Type  Flags  Property Tag  Quoted CA Value / URI
example.com.      3600   IN     CAA   0      issue         "letsencrypt.org"
example.com.      3600   IN     CAA   0      issuewild     ";"
example.com.      3600   IN     CAA   0      iodef         "mailto:security@example.com"</code></pre>

      <p class="text-sm font-medium text-ink-dim">
        The table below details the 3 data fields and standardized property tags comprising every DNS CAA resource record:
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
        <table class="pdns-table w-full text-left text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-line text-ink font-semibold">
              <th class="py-2.5 px-3">CAA Component</th>
              <th class="py-2.5 px-3">Valid Values / Example</th>
              <th class="py-2.5 px-3">Function in Certificate Issuance Validation</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-line text-ink-dim">
            <tr>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3 font-medium text-ink">Field 1: Flags (0 or 128)</td>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3 font-mono text-xs">0 (Default) or 128 (Issuer Critical)</td>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3">8-bit integer: 0 is standard non-critical; 128 sets Issuer Critical, instructing CAs to abort on unrecognized tags.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3 font-medium text-ink">Tag 1: issue</td>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3 font-mono text-xs">"letsencrypt.org" or ";"</td>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3">Authorizes single-domain certificates (and wildcards if issuewild is omitted).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3 font-medium text-ink">Tag 2: issuewild</td>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3 font-mono text-xs">"digicert.com" or ";"</td>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3">Governs <abbr class="pd-term" data-tip="Wildcard certificate: public SSL/TLS certificate valid for all immediate subdomains (*.example.com)">wildcard certificates</abbr>. An <abbr class="pd-term" data-tip="issuewild: CAA property tag exclusively controlling wildcard certificate issuance permissions">issuewild</abbr> tag overrides all issue records for wildcard requests.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3 font-medium text-ink">Tag 3: iodef</td>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3 font-mono text-xs">"mailto:sec@example.com" or "https://..."</td>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3"><abbr class="pd-term" data-tip="iodef: Incident Object Description Exchange Format tag specifying email or URL endpoints for policy violation alerts">Incident Object Description Exchange Format</abbr>: specifies email or URL endpoints for violation alerts.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3 font-medium text-ink">Tag 4: issueemail (RFC 9495)</td>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3 font-mono text-xs">"sectigo.com" or ";"</td>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3">Controls which CAs issue S/MIME client email certificates.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3 font-medium text-ink">ACME Binding Parameters (RFC 8657)</td>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3 font-mono text-xs">"letsencrypt.org; accounturi=...; validationmethods=dns-01"</td>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3">Locks issuance to a specific <abbr class="pd-term" data-tip="ACME: Automated Certificate Management Environment protocol enabling automated TLS certificate issuance">ACME</abbr> account URI and validation method.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        CAA operates open-by-default: domains lacking CAA records permit every public Certificate Authority to issue certificates. Publishing a single <code>issue</code> record (such as <code>0 issue "letsencrypt.org"</code>) creates an immediate implicit deny-all restriction against every other CA globally. Administrators inspect live issuance constraints using the <a href="/caa-record-lookup/" class="text-accent underline hover:text-accent-hover">CAA Record Lookup</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="official-caa-domain-identifiers-for-8-major-certificate-authorities-and-cdns">Official CAA Domain Identifiers for 8 Major Certificate Authorities and CDNs</h2>
      <p>
        Publishing a CAA record requires entering <strong class="text-ink">the exact ASCII domain identifier recognized by your SSL vendor, cloud load balancer, or CDN edge proxy</strong>. The table below lists the official CAA Value strings for 8 major Certificate Authorities and cloud platforms.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
        <table class="pdns-table w-full text-left text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-line text-ink font-semibold">
              <th class="py-2.5 px-3">Certificate Authority / Cloud Platform</th>
              <th class="py-2.5 px-3">Official CAA Value String(s)</th>
              <th class="py-2.5 px-3">Important Platform Notes</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-line text-ink-dim">
            <tr>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3 font-medium text-ink">Let's Encrypt</td>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3 font-mono text-xs">"letsencrypt.org"</td>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3">Standard for certbot, ACME automation, Vercel, Netlify, and Render.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3 font-medium text-ink">Google Trust Services (GTS)</td>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3 font-mono text-xs">"pki.goog"</td>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3">Primary CA for Google Cloud, Firebase, and Cloudflare.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3 font-medium text-ink">DigiCert (Includes GeoTrust / Thawte)</td>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3 font-mono text-xs">"digicert.com"</td>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3">Enterprise OV/EV certificates and DigiCert CertCentral.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3 font-medium text-ink">Sectigo (Formerly Comodo CA)</td>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3 font-mono text-xs">"sectigo.com" (or "comodoca.com")</td>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3">cPanel AutoSSL, Namecheap PositiveSSL, and enterprise PKI.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3 font-medium text-ink">ZeroSSL</td>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3 font-mono text-xs">"zerossl.com" and "sectigo.com"</td>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3">Issues through Sectigo roots; setups authorize both.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3 font-medium text-ink">AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)</td>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3 font-mono text-xs">"amazon.com"</td>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3">Required for AWS ALBs, CloudFront, and API Gateways.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3 font-medium text-ink">SSL.com</td>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3 font-mono text-xs">"ssl.com"</td>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3">Commercial certificates and backup CA for Cloudflare.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3 font-medium text-ink">Cloudflare Universal SSL (Proxied)</td>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3 font-mono text-xs">"letsencrypt.org", "pki.goog", "ssl.com", "digicert.com"</td>
              <td class="py-2.5 px-3">Cloudflare provisions edge certificates across these 4 CAs.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        Deployments pairing origin certificates with edge proxies require dual authorization. Origin servers running <a href="/free-ssl-lets-encrypt/" class="text-accent underline hover:text-accent-hover">Let's Encrypt: Free Certificates and ACME Automation</a> (<code>0 issue "letsencrypt.org"</code>) behind Cloudflare or CloudFront require administrators to authorize both origin and CDN CAs (<code>pki.goog</code> or <code>amazon.com</code>) on the apex domain. Omitting CDN identifiers causes edge certificate renewals to fail after 90 days. Trust hierarchy mechanics are detailed in <a href="/certificate-authorities-explained/" class="text-accent underline hover:text-accent-hover">Certificate Authorities and the Web of Trust</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="how-do-certificate-authorities-evaluate-caa-records-tree-climbing-inheritance-and-overrides">How Do Certificate Authorities Evaluate CAA Records? (Tree-Climbing Inheritance and Overrides)</h2>
      <p>
        Validation servers execute <strong class="text-ink">the RFC 8659 Tree-Climbing Algorithm across 5 lookup steps from the specific subdomain up to the zone apex (@) whenever evaluating certificate requests</strong>:
      </p>

      <ol class="my-4 space-y-3 pl-6 list-decimal text-ink-dim">
        <li>
          <strong class="text-ink">Query</strong> the exact hostname (<code>sub.app.example.com</code>) for a CAA record; finding records terminates the climb to evaluate only those entries.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-ink">Follow</strong> any <a href="/cname-record/" class="text-accent underline hover:text-accent-hover">CNAME Record</a> or DNAME alias attached to <code>sub.app.example.com</code> to check for a CAA record at the exact canonical target hostname; if no CAA record exists at that target, RFC 8659 climbs the original requested domain's parent tree (app.example.com -> example.com) rather than the CNAME target's parent tree.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-ink">Climb</strong> one label up to parent subdomain (<code>app.example.com</code>) and query for CAA records when the child label returns no records.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-ink">Continue</strong> climbing upward to the apex root (<code>example.com</code>, <code>@</code>) and evaluate root CAA records when intermediate labels lack records.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-ink">Approve</strong> issuance upon finding a matching <code>issue</code> or <code>issuewild</code> tag or zero CAA records; abort issuance upon finding a set omitting that CA.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        Child subdomain CAA records completely override parent root CAA records rather than merging with them. A root domain (<code>example.com</code>) allowing only <code>"digicert.com"</code> permits publishing <code>dev.example.com. IN CAA 0 issue "letsencrypt.org"</code> on a staging subdomain. That single child record replaces the root allowlist for <code>dev.example.com</code>, enabling Let's Encrypt for staging while keeping DigiCert enforced everywhere else.
      </p>

      <h2 id="5-copy-paste-caa-record-examples-for-every-security-scenario">5 Copy-Paste CAA Record Examples for Every Security Scenario</h2>
      <p>
        Administrators combine <code>issue</code>, <code>issuewild</code>, <code>iodef</code>, and empty semicolon (<code>";"</code>) values into <strong class="text-ink">5 standard CAA record configurations ranging from single-CA lockdown to wildcard prevention and ACME account pinning</strong>:
      </p>

      <ol class="my-4 space-y-4 pl-6 list-decimal text-ink-dim">
        <li>
          <strong class="text-ink">Recipe 1: Authorize Only Let's Encrypt for Both Single-Domain and Wildcard Certificates:</strong>
          <pre class="mt-2 overflow-x-auto rounded border border-line bg-panel/60 p-4 font-mono text-xs text-ink leading-relaxed"><code>example.com.  3600  IN  CAA  0 issue "letsencrypt.org"</code></pre>
          A single <code>issue</code> record covers both single-domain and <code>*.example.com</code> wildcard certificates when <code>issuewild</code> is omitted.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-ink">Recipe 2: Allow Single-Domain Certificates While Blocking ALL Wildcard Certificates (<code>0 issuewild ";"</code>):</strong>
          <pre class="mt-2 overflow-x-auto rounded border border-line bg-panel/60 p-4 font-mono text-xs text-ink leading-relaxed"><code>example.com.  3600  IN  CAA  0 issue "letsencrypt.org"
example.com.  3600  IN  CAA  0 issuewild ";"
example.com.  3600  IN  CAA  0 iodef "mailto:security@example.com"</code></pre>
          Setting <code>issuewild</code> to a semicolon forbids wildcard certificate issuance (<code>*.example.com</code>), auditable via the <a href="/wildcard-dns-tester/" class="text-accent underline hover:text-accent-hover">Wildcard DNS Tester</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-ink">Recipe 3: Multi-CA Production Setup (Origin Let's Encrypt + AWS CloudFront ACM + DigiCert):</strong>
          <pre class="mt-2 overflow-x-auto rounded border border-line bg-panel/60 p-4 font-mono text-xs text-ink leading-relaxed"><code>example.com.  3600  IN  CAA  0 issue "letsencrypt.org"
example.com.  3600  IN  CAA  0 issue "amazon.com"
example.com.  3600  IN  CAA  0 issue "digicert.com"</code></pre>
          Multi-CA architectures authorize providers across separate record rows, ensuring edge and origin renewals proceed concurrently.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-ink">Recipe 4: Pin Let's Encrypt Issuance to Your Exact ACME Account URI (RFC 8657):</strong>
          <pre class="mt-2 overflow-x-auto rounded border border-line bg-panel/60 p-4 font-mono text-xs text-ink leading-relaxed"><code>example.com.  3600  IN  CAA  0 issue "letsencrypt.org; accounturi=https://acme-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/acme/acct/123456789; validationmethods=dns-01"</code></pre>
          Account binding prevents compromised servers from requesting certificates under alternative ACME accounts.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-ink">Recipe 5: Total Certificate Lockdown for Parked or Non-Web Domains (<code>0 issue ";"</code>):</strong>
          <pre class="mt-2 overflow-x-auto rounded border border-line bg-panel/60 p-4 font-mono text-xs text-ink leading-relaxed"><code>parked-example.com.  86400  IN  CAA  0 issue ";"</code></pre>
          A semicolon inside the <code>issue</code> tag forbids all Certificate Authorities from issuing certificates for the domain.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        Authorizing multiple CAs requires separate record rows rather than comma-separated lists. Core transport security principles are explained in <a href="/what-is-ssl-tls/" class="text-accent underline hover:text-accent-hover">What Is SSL/TLS</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="why-do-caa-lookups-cause-ssl-certificate-renewal-failures-3-common-pitfalls">Why Do CAA Lookups Cause SSL Certificate Renewal Failures? (3 Common Pitfalls)</h2>
      <p>
        Automated certbot or CDN certificate renewals fail with a CAA error due to <strong class="text-ink">3 DNS configuration traps: missing CDN authority strings, issuewild overrides, and DNSSEC SERVFAIL timeouts</strong>:
      </p>

      <ol class="my-4 space-y-3 pl-6 list-decimal text-ink-dim">
        <li>
          <strong class="text-ink">SERVFAIL on a CAA Query Forces CAs to Abort Issuance:</strong> Under CA/Browser Forum rules, an empty response (<code>NOERROR</code> with 0 records) permits issuance. Nameserver timeouts or broken <a href="/what-is-dnssec/" class="text-accent underline hover:text-accent-hover">What Is DNSSEC</a> chains returning <code>SERVFAIL</code> (<code>RCODE 2</code>) legally compel CAs to abort issuance because servers cannot confirm whether a restrictive CAA record was concealed by the failure. Administrators audit validation health using the <a href="/dnssec-checker/" class="text-accent underline hover:text-accent-hover">DNSSEC Checker</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-ink">Adding an issuewild Record for One CA Blocks Other Providers:</strong> Publishing an <code>issuewild</code> record overrides all <code>issue</code> records for wildcard requests (<code>*.example.com</code>). Adding <code>0 issuewild "amazon.com"</code> to a zone containing <code>0 issue "letsencrypt.org"</code> immediately blocks Let's Encrypt from issuing wildcard certificates.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-ink">Blocking Cloudflare or PaaS Managed Certificates:</strong> Publishing strict CAA records on the apex (<code>@</code>) without including CAs used by cloud hosts (like Vercel, Render, Shopify, or Cloudflare) triggers silent renewal failures and <a href="/ssl-certificate-errors/" class="text-accent underline hover:text-accent-hover">SSL Certificate Errors</a> when certificates expire.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        Proactive CAA auditing prevents unexpected web outages. Comprehensive security architecture is detailed in <a href="/ssl-tls/" class="text-accent underline hover:text-accent-hover">SSL/TLS and Web Security Basics</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="how-to-add-and-check-your-domains-caa-records-dig-nslookup-and-browser-doh">How to Add and Check Your Domain's CAA Records (dig, nslookup, and Browser DoH)</h2>
      <p>
        To verify which Certificate Authorities are authorized on your domain and test subdomain inheritance, run <strong class="text-ink">a browser DoH lookup or terminal dig query across 3 steps</strong>:
      </p>

      <ol class="my-4 space-y-3 pl-6 list-decimal text-ink-dim">
        <li>
          <strong class="text-ink">Query</strong> root domain (<code>@</code>) and custom subdomain CAA records from your terminal using <code>dig</code> or <code>nslookup</code>:
          <pre class="mt-2 overflow-x-auto rounded border border-line bg-panel/60 p-3 font-mono text-xs text-ink leading-relaxed"><code># 1. Query root domain CAA records on Linux / macOS:
dig example.com CAA +short

# 2. Query CAA records on Windows Command Prompt (Type 257):
nslookup -type=caa example.com 1.1.1.1</code></pre>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-ink">Confirm</strong> that every CA utilized by your origin servers, CDN edge layers, and subdomains appears in the output prior to certificate renewal windows.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-ink">Check</strong> that newly published CAA records propagate across global recursive resolvers using the <a href="/dns-propagation-checker/" class="text-accent underline hover:text-accent-hover">DNS Propagation Checker</a>.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        Auditing certificate issuance policies preserves transport encryption health across the broader directory of <a href="/dns-records/" class="text-accent underline hover:text-accent-hover">DNS Record Types</a>. Every production CAA record (IANA Type 257) anchors public key infrastructure by enforcing explicit allowlists across Flags 0 and 128, issue, issuewild, and iodef tags. Establishing multi-CA authorizations for origin and CDN platforms, leveraging tree-climbing inheritance on subdomains, and verifying <abbr class="pd-term" data-tip="DNSSEC: Domain Name System Security Extensions protecting DNS data with cryptographic signatures">DNSSEC</abbr> resolution shields domains from unauthorized certificate minting.
      </p>

      <h2 id="frequently-asked-questions">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <div class="space-y-4 my-6">
        <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/40 p-4 transition-colors open:bg-panel/70">
          <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between font-medium text-ink">
            <span>What happens if my domain does not have a CAA record?</span>
            <span class="ml-2 text-ink-faint transition-transform group-open:rotate-180">▾</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
            <p>A domain with zero CAA records permits any publicly trusted Certificate Authority to issue an SSL/TLS certificate provided the requester passes standard domain validation (HTTP-01 or DNS-01). Publishing a CAA record is optional, but strongly recommended to lock issuance down to your chosen CA.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/40 p-4 transition-colors open:bg-panel/70">
          <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between font-medium text-ink">
            <span>How do I allow multiple Certificate Authorities in a CAA record?</span>
            <span class="ml-2 text-ink-faint transition-transform group-open:rotate-180">▾</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
            <p>To authorize more than one Certificate Authority (for example, both Let's Encrypt and DigiCert), create separate CAA record rows on the same hostname: one row with 0 issue "letsencrypt.org" and a second row with 0 issue "digicert.com". Never put multiple CA names inside a single CAA record.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/40 p-4 transition-colors open:bg-panel/70">
          <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between font-medium text-ink">
            <span>Does a CAA record on my root domain (@) automatically protect all of my subdomains?</span>
            <span class="ml-2 text-ink-faint transition-transform group-open:rotate-180">▾</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
            <p>Yes, in practice CAA records automatically inherit downward to all subdomains via tree climbing (sub.example.com inherits example.com's CAA rules). Adding a single CAA record directly onto a subdomain completely replaces the root domain's CAA list for that subdomain rather than merging with it.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/40 p-4 transition-colors open:bg-panel/70">
          <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between font-medium text-ink">
            <span>Why did Let's Encrypt fail with "SERVFAIL looking up CAA" when I don't even have a CAA record?</span>
            <span class="ml-2 text-ink-faint transition-transform group-open:rotate-180">▾</span>
          </summary>
          <div class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
            <p>In real-world troubleshooting, this error means Let's Encrypt queried your domain for a CAA record and received a DNS error (SERVFAIL or timeout). Broken DNSSEC DS records at your registrar or unresponsive nameservers trigger this error. Because CAs verify whether a CAA record exists, a DNS SERVFAIL blocks issuance until administrators repair the DNS or DNSSEC chain.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
